ttributable to the optimizer's internal op structure.\n\n4. METRICS\n4.1 Graph-complexity metrics (countable, computed by programmatic DOT parsing \u2014 graphviz JSON export or pygraphviz; NOT manual counting):\n   - Total node count (tensor nodes + op nodes).\n   - Op-node count and edge count.\n   - Longest-path depth of the traced graph.\n   - Number of distinct op types (label vocabulary for ops).\n   - Per-parameter-tensor node ratio (nodes divided by number of parameter tensors), which normalizes for model width.\n   - Serialized DOT size in bytes (coarse proxy, reported but not load-bearing).\n   - Optimizer-subgraph share: metrics above recomputed after subtracting the shared forward/backward subgraph (identical across conditions) \u2014 see \u00a75 isolation methods.\n4.2 Interpretability metrics:\n   - Countable proxies: fraction of nodes carrying human-readable op labels vs. fused/anonymous or in-place-opaque labels; distinct label vocabulary size; number of intermediate nodes a reader must traverse to explain one parameter's update path; number of update-related nodes per parameter tensor.\n   - Qualitative rubric (pre-registered, applied to rendered SVG/PNG of each graph):\n     R1: Can a reader locate the bias-correction step (1 \u2212 \u03b2^t division)? (yes/no)\n     R2: Can the Adam update equation be reconstructed from node labels alone? (full/partial/no)\n     R3: Are node names self-describing (op names with shapes) vs. anonymous (e.g., generic fused call sites)? (mostly/mixed/mostly anonymous)\n     R4: Overall rendered-graph readability on a 1\u20135 Likert scale, scored against the rubric before rendering any graphs (pre-registration ordering: rubric first, graphs second).\n   - Baseline: Condition A defines the interpretability reference; claims of \"more interpretable\" require C (or B) to score at least as well on R1\u2013R3 and better on the countable proxies, not merely to be smaller.\n4.3 Conditions/comparisons: primary B vs C (fusion effect); secondary A vs B (functional-API framing effect); A serves as baseline for both complexity and interpretability.\n\n5. ISOLATING THE OPTIMIZER SUBGRAPH (two complementary methods, both reported)\n   Method 1 \u2014 Subtraction: since the forward/backward subgraph is identical across conditions (same seed, model, batch), differences in node/edge counts isolate the optimizer region. Shared nodes are matched by structural position.\n   Method 2 \u2014 Update-only dummy trace: trace `L_upd = \u03a3 updated_params\u00b2` so the graph contains essentially only the update computation; counts are then directly attributable to optimizer ops. The two methods are expected to agree; if they disagree, both numbers are reported and the discrepancy is discussed rather than resolved silently.\n\n6. ASSUMPTION AND UNCERTAINTY REGISTER\n   Assumptions (to be verified at execution time, not treated as facts):\n   - A1: TorchOpt exposes both fused and unfused Adam variants toggleable via a single API. Candidate API: `torchopt.adam(..., inplace=...)` (fused in-place path) vs `inplace=False` functional path; exact flag name and semantics differ across TorchOpt releases (some versions use `inplace`, newer versions restructure the functional API), so the pinned version's docs govern.\n   - A2: `torchviz.make_dot` labels autograd nodes by op name, so fused updates, IF they record as single coarser autograd nodes, should appear as fewer nodes with less granular labels.\n   - A3: Fixed-seed CPU execution makes the forward/backward subgraph identical across conditions.\n   - A4: DOT parsing captures all nodes/edges needed for the metrics (no graphviz rendering truncation at these graph sizes).\n   Uncertainties (empirical outcomes, not assumed away):\n   - U1 (load-bearing): PyTorch autograd may internally expand fused/in-place ops into primitive backward nodes. If so, the fused condition C may show little or no node-count reduction, or even *more* opaque nodes with less informative labels \u2014 the design explicitly allows this outcome and treats it as a legitimate answer to the research question (\"fusing reduces conceptual op count but the traced autograd graph may not reflect it\").\n   - U2: Version drift in both TorchOpt and torchviz could change node labeling conventions; mitigated by pinning versions (see \u00a77) and reporting them with all results.\n   - U3: The two subgraph-isolation methods (\u00a75) may disagree; both are reported.\n   - U4: Interpretability is partly subjective; claims are therefore limited to countable label statistics plus the documented rubric, not a generalized claim of \"understandability.\"\n   - U5: Generalization limits \u2014 a single small model, a single optimizer (Adam), a single step, CPU-only, and one visualization tool (torchviz). Findings would be scoped to \"this setup\" and would not automatically extend to other optimizers, larger models, GPU execution (where fused kernels may trace differently), or other visualization tools.\n\n7. ENVIRONMENT AND VERSION PINNING (documented, since network is disabled in the design environment)\n   - Candidate pinned versions (to be confirmed against locally available wheels at execution time; nothing is claimed as verified): torch \u2265 2.x CPU build; torchopt (any version whose docs confirm the fused/inplace Adam flag; record exact version string); torchviz (`make_dot`); graphviz Python binding + system `dot` for rendering/parsing.\n   - Exact API calls to be recorded in the execution script header, e.g.:\n       optimizer = torchopt.adam(lr=1e-2, inplace=False)   # Condition B\n       optimizer = torchopt.adam(lr=1e-2, inplace=True)    # Condition C\n       updates = torchopt.apply_updates(params, grads, optimizer)   # names per pinned docs\n     If the pinned version's flag names differ (e.g., fused path exposed differently), the deviation is documented in the register rather than silently adapted.\n   - Offline-prepared environment option: because the design environment has network disabled and no ML packages installed, execution would require either (i) a pre-provisioned image with the pinned wheels (torch CPU, torchopt, torchviz, pygraphviz, graphviz system package) or (ii) vendored wheels placed in /workspace before execution. This design does not depend on which provisioning route is used; it only requires that versions are recorded.\n\n8. ANALYSIS PLAN (pre-registered hypotheses, no results claimed)\n   - Outcome patterns that would SUPPORT \"less complex and more interpretable\": Condition C shows materially lower op-node/edge counts and depth in the optimizer subgraph than A and B (by both isolation methods), a smaller fraction of labeled primitive-op nodes, fewer nodes per parameter tensor, and equal-or-better rubric scores R1\u2013R3.\n   - Patterns that would REFUTE the claim: no meaningful reduction in C vs B on complexity metrics, or lower interpretability scores in C (e.g., anonymous fused nodes that obscure the update equation, failing R2), consistent with U1.\n   - Patterns that would QUALIFY the claim: complexity reduced but interpretability degraded (smaller graph, less reconstructible) \u2014 this would yield the qualified conclusion \"fusing reduces graph size but not readability under torchviz,\" which is a substantive answer to the question as posed.\n   - Decision rule: the primary claim is evaluated on B vs C for complexity metrics AND interpretability proxies jointly; A vs B is reported as descriptive context only. Effect sizes (ratios/absolute deltas) are reported rather than binary verdicts; no significance testing is required given n=1 deterministic trace per condition (a limitation, acknowledged).\n   - All three DOT outputs and their rendered images would be archived as artifacts so claims are auditable.\n\n9. FEASIBILITY (stated, not executed)\n   - Runtime/compute: trivially small \u2014 one forward/backward pass plus one Adam update per condition on a tiny MLP; seconds of CPU time; no GPU required; memory well under 1 GB. Total wall-clock for all three conditions including rendering: minutes.\n   - Provisioning needed: pinned CPU builds of torch, torchopt, torchviz, pygraphviz/graphviz (see \u00a77), pre-installed offline.\n   - Executable in principle: yes \u2014 every step (seeding, three update variants, make_dot tracing, DOT parsing, rendering, rubric scoring) is a standard, well-understood operation; the only genuine empirical unknowns are those registered in \u00a76 (especially U1), which the design is constructed to measure rather than assume away.\n   - This design document makes no claim that any of the above was run.\n\n10. LIMITATIONS ON GENERALIZATION\n   Single small model architecture; single optimizer (Adam); single update step; CPU only (fused kernels on GPU may record differently in autograd); one visualization tool (torchviz) \u2014 the question's framing ties \"output of TorchViz\" to torchviz's rendering, so conclusions do not transfer to other graph visualizers without re-running the same protocol; interpretability judgments are rubric-scoped, not universal.", "evidence": "Repository/environment observations supporting this design (read-only checks only; no experiment executed):\n- /workspace contains exactly one file, task.json (466 bytes), restating the design-only question and referencing a \"track.md\" (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\") that is not present. No TorchOpt/TorchViz source, docs, or configs exist locally.\n- python3 import checks: torch -> ModuleNotFoundError; torchopt -> not installed (implied by torch failure and absent from pip list); graphviz -> ModuleNotFoundError; `dot` binary not found. pip3 list contains only unrelated system packages (ansible, dbus-python, Jinja2, etc.).\n- These checks justify: (i) designing from library knowledge alone, stated explicitly; (ii) the offline environment-provisioning requirement in \u00a77; (iii) the version/API pinning register in \u00a76 (A1/U2), since no local source exists to verify the inplace-flag semantics.\nDesign integrity points preserved from the approved plan: three fixed conditions A/B/C traced with the same tool (torchviz.make_dot) on an identical fixed-seed model/batch; B-vs-C as the primary fusion-effect comparison with A as baseline; two complementary optimizer-subgraph isolation methods (shared-subgraph subtraction and update-only dummy trace) with both reported; programmatic DOT parsing for all countable metrics; pre-registered rubric (R1\u2013R4, rubric defined before rendering) plus countable label proxies for interpretability; explicit register including the U1 uncertainty that autograd may expand fused/in-place ops so fusion need not reduce node count; explicit no-execution/no-fabrication status; feasibility section confirming small CPU-only runtime and executable-in-principle status.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver final three-condition TorchViz graph comparison design", "uncertainties": ["U1 (load-bearing): autograd may expand fused/in-place TorchOpt ops into primitive backward nodes, so Condition C may show little or no node-count reduction, or reduced label informativeness \u2014 the analysis plan treats refutation and qualification patterns as legitimate outcomes.", "A1/U2: the exact TorchOpt fused/unfused Adam API (inplace flag naming and semantics) varies across releases and cannot be verified locally (no source, no package installed); the design mandates pinning and documenting the version actually used.", "U3: the two subgraph-isolation methods (shared-subgraph subtraction vs update-only dummy trace) may produce different counts; both are reported, discrepancies discussed.", "U4: interpretability is partly subjective; conclusions are limited to countable label proxies plus the pre-registered rubric, not universal readability claims.", "U5: generalization is limited to one small model, one optimizer (Adam), one update step, CPU execution, and torchviz as the single visualization tool; GPU fused kernels and other visualizers may behave differently.", "Environment provisioning: network is disabled and no ML packages are installed, so execution requires pre-provisioned pinned wheels or vendored dependencies; this design documents but cannot verify that provisioning."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-70de0944396e47dbbc8cc18858165746", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver final three-condition TorchViz graph comparison design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
